{{smaller block|Never [was] there such an ''amphibolous'' quarrel—both parties declaring themselves for the king.
{{right|''Howell'' (?), England’s Tears.}}}}
{{smaller block|An ''amphibolous'' sentence is one that is capable of two meanings, not from the double sense of any of the words, but from its admitting a double construction; as, . . . “The duke yet lives that Henry shall depose.”
{{right|''Whately'', Logic, iii. ¶ 10.}}}}

'''Amphibolura''' (am″fi-bō-lū′rä), ''n.'' [NL. (Cabanis, 1847), < Gr. ἀμφίβολος, doubtful, ambiguous (see ''amphibole''), + οὐρά, tail.] In ''ornith.'', the corrected orthography of ''Phibalura'' (which see). [Not in use.]

'''amphiboly''' (am-fib′ō-li), ''n.''; pl. ''amphibolies'' (-liz). [< L. ''amphibolia'', < Gr. ἀμφιβολία, ambiguity, < ἀμφίβολος, ambiguous: see ''amphibole''.] '''1.''' The use of ambiguities; quibbling.—'''2.''' In ''logic'', ambiguity in the meaning of a proposition, arising either from an uncertain syntax or from a figure of speech.—{{smaller|'''Transcendental amphiboly,''' in the ''Kantian philosophy'', the confusing of conceptions which exist in the understanding a priori (categories) with those which are derived from experience.}}

